{"text": "Since hydra and the immortal jellyfish do not exhibit obvious signs of aging, they are often called biologically immortal. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> This does not mean they do not die. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Nor does it even mean that they cannot die of biological causes. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> But, unlike most every animal, their likelihood of dying does not increase with age. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Species such as hydra and the immortal jellyfish excite gerontologists because they may provide clues about how to defeat the aging process. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> But their property of being able to regenerate entire body parts, or even a whole organism, makes them more similar to trees than to us. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Although we may learn some fascinating things about their lack of apparent aging, it is not at all clear how relevant those findings will be to human aging. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> Sometimes biology is universal, especially if it relates to fundamental mechanisms. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> But in other cases, even discoveries in rats or mice, which are mammals and biologically much closer to us, are difficult to translate into humans. <break time=\"0.8s\" /> It may be a very long time before any findings gained from hydra or jellyfish are useful to us.", "alignment": {"characters": ["S", "i", "n", "c", "e", " ", "h", "y", "d", "r", "a", " ", "a", "n", "d", " ", "t", "h", "e", " ", "i", "m", "m", "o", "r", "t", "a", "l", " ", "j", "e", "l", "l", "y", "f", "i", "s", "h", " ", "d", "o", " ", "n", "o", "t", " ", "e", "x", "h", "i", "b", "i", "t", " ", "o", "b", "v", "i", "o", "u", "s", " ", "s", "i", "g", "n", "s", " ", "o", "f", " ", "a", "g", "i", "n", "g", ",", " ", "t", "h", "e", "y", " ", "a", "r", "e", " ", "o", "f", "t", "e", "n", " ", "c", "a", "l", "l", "e", "d", " ", "b", "i", "o", "l", "o", "g", "i", "c", "a", "l", "l", "y", " ", "i", "m", "m", "o", "r", "t", "a", "l", ".", " ", "<", "b", "r", "e", "a", "k", " ", "t", "i", "m", "e", "